abstract="Most digital cultural heritage applications still follow a ``content-overlay'' AR paradigm, superimposing predefined information onto physical sites for presentation and guided tours. This limits the narrative potential of physical media and constrains public agency in meaning-making. While prior work improves scene visualization and immersive tours, narratives remain expert-defined and hard to scale for open collaboration. To enable co-creative heritage storytelling, we designed and evaluated ``Dynamic Mosaic,'' a collaborative AR narrative system, and propose a generative placemaking interaction framework coupling ``content generation---place experience---public participation.'' Traditional mosaic craft was translated into a computational cultural interface as both spatial anchor and semantic base. In our experiment, physical mosaics triggered virtual characters, while participants uploaded images, texts, and story fragments; AIGC extended regionally grounded narratives that were assembled into dynamic semantic networks on a shared interface. Following a design science research paradigm, we recruited 80 participants (40 pairs; 48 female, 32 male; mean age 24.7, SD 3.5) for a simulation study with three conditions: physical-only (26), pure AR (26), and hybrid (28). Linear mixed-effects modeling showed the hybrid condition significantly outperformed single-media conditions in cultural learning, collaboration quality, and sense of place, yielding {\textasciitilde}30{\%} higher collaboration quality with no added cognitive load. Behavioral analysis further revealed spontaneous content re-creation beyond assigned tasks. We contribute a validated generative placemaking framework integrating traditional craft, AIGC, and AR, an experimental pipeline for collaborative narrative generation, and design evidence for transforming historic urban spaces into sustainable citizen-participatory digital cultural platforms.",
isbn="978-3-032-30549-7"
